Windows/ntlibc changes to gcc-core-4.6.4's own bundled libiberty/ -- a
different, older copy than ../binutils/windows.patch's (binutils 2.46's own
libiberty), so a separate patch, same pattern: `#if defined(_WIN32)' written
against a real Win32 CRT (MAX_PATH, DWORD, GetFullPathName, GetTempPath,
GlobalMemoryStatus-equivalent) that ntlibc does not have, because ntlibc is
a from-scratch POSIX layer over NT, not a port of the MSVC/mingw runtime.
tcc predefines _WIN32 (TCC_TARGET_PE says so -- see conftest.c in the tcc
fork), so every one of these branches is taken and fails to compile as-is.

Same `&& !defined(__TINYC__)' guard as binutils' own patch, for the same
reason: this chain has exactly one compiler, so naming it directly is more
honest than pretending a real mingw distinction is being drawn. Every
branch guarded off has a working POSIX alternative right next to it, and
ntlibc satisfies it for real, checked directly against ntlibc's headers,
not assumed:

  libiberty/lrealpath.c       lrealpath()'s _WIN32 GetFullPathName method
                               (line 137) is genuinely dead code for this
                               target, not just guarded off: HAVE_REALPATH=1
                               (ntlibc/include/stdlib.h has a real realpath())
                               means Method 3 (pathconf/realpath, a few lines
                               above) always returns first. The earlier
                               `#if defined (_WIN32)' at line 69 needs no
                               guard -- it lives inside `#else' of
                               `#if defined(HAVE_REALPATH)', already false.
  libiberty/make-temp-file.c  choose_tmpdir's GetTempPath route. The POSIX
                               branch (TMPDIR/TMP/TEMP, P_tmpdir, /var/tmp,
                               /usr/tmp, /tmp) is complete on its own --
                               getenv/access, both real on ntlibc -- and,
                               unlike lrealpath.c, this one is not just dead
                               code: without the guard ntlibc's build would
                               genuinely try to call GetTempPath at runtime.
  libiberty/physmem.c         physmem_total/physmem_available's
                               GlobalMemoryStatus-equivalent route, three
                               call sites (the struct/typedef above and both
                               functions). ntlibc's unistd.h defines
                               _SC_PHYS_PAGES and _SC_PAGESIZE (checked
                               directly), so physmem_total's sysconf()
                               branch, already at the top of the function,
                               is the one that runs. physmem_available finds
                               no _SC_AVPHYS_PAGES in ntlibc (checked: not
                               defined), so it falls through every guarded
                               branch to the function's own final fallback
                               -- an accepted, harmless degradation, same as
                               every other unsupported-host case already in
                               this file (HAVE_PSTAT_GETSTATIC,
                               HAVE_SYSMP, HAVE_TABLE, HAVE_SYSCTL, all
                               already undef for this target); nothing in
                               this bootstrap reads physmem_available for
                               correctness.

libiberty/fnmatch.c needs no patch, a shim instead: `#include <fnmatch.h>'
resolves against ntlibc's own real (but GNU-extension-free) fnmatch.h,
which has no FNM_CASEFOLD -- same gap, same fix as binutils' own
../binutils/shim/fnmatch.h (byte-for-byte copy, same reasoning: ntlibc's
fnmatch() itself is a correct POSIX one and its three POSIX flag bit values
already agree with gnulib's, checked before reusing the file verbatim).
gcc-core's libiberty/fnmatch.c defines its own fnmatch() (not gnulib's
rpl_fnmatch) but only needs the FNM_CASEFOLD constant from this header,
same as before -- the object file, not the header, is what's actually
linked.

